{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/342946-how-do-you-make-a-chronicle-work","id":342946,"title":"How do you make a chronicle work?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-17T08:25:48-08:00","id":342946,"title":"How do you make a chronicle work?","content":"I've always been interested in Wraith and now seems like a good time to really try and do something more than just reading the material. What I can't really grasp is how do you actually make a game run. Seems to me like a pretty hard thing to do to balance all personal stuff (all those fetters and unresolved issues) with a main plot or storyline. How are 4-6 players supposed to care for each other or a story involving legions, the hierarchy, far shores and what not when they all seem to have far more important personal matters involving their shadows and past lives? Can this game even be played with such a large group and retain its essence?<br /> <br /> I can sort of see how the &quot;you all died on a plane crash&quot; sort of fib can help. However it only concerns the manner of death, not personal business of every player. Also I don't want to force them to all have just one manner of death; I think it would be better for each of them to personally flesh out the characters at their leisure.<br /> <br /> On another note... How do you deal with illegal archanoi? In some ways they seem like the most interesting things a wraith can do and yet if any player takes them they are supposed to not use them or face retribution each time they do?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4106-caspersgu","name":"caspersgu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-17T09:27:35-08:00","id":343039,"title":"","content":"I DID the &quot;you all die in a plane crash&quot; thing and it worked really well, with the hiccup of how to deal with &quot;apprentice level&quot; wraiths, as the default assumption is that the game is about wraiths who have a clue, have learned Arcanoi, aren't minutes/days out of their cauls.. <br /> <br /> You are going to have to have SOMETHING really tight to bind a circle of wraiths together to run a game. The mechanics really promote a lot of very definitely self-interest (MY Passions, MY Fetters, MY Shadow...) A shared death is one way to do it. All being in the same Legion accomplishes the same thing (though the two are relatively equivalent in many ways). Another group was put together as an action team by a &quot;Special Circumstances Group&quot; within the Hierarchy to deal with a particular threat to the Necropolis and each had some reason valid to his/her character as to why they were a functional contributing member of the group. <br /> <br /> As many have pointed out, Wraith can go so many many many ways... Skinland adjacent pining for one's Passions &amp; Fetters, hand-wringing Shadow battling internalized psychodramas,, specter-fighters, Hierarchy invested Legionnaires (stooges?), SUPER-specter fighters aka Doomslayers, Stygian politicos, cross-over or diplomat or aggressive diplomats dealing with other Dark Kingdoms, etc. etc. And all the ways those can twist and combine. If you want to run a game of Wraith you have got to also figure out what kind of Wraith game you are going to run. Or at least start with. <br /> <br /> Both games I've run have been Skinland adjacent Necropolis games with a strong Hierarchy focus. So I had to do my heavy lifting up front to get all the major Hierarchy players and other relevant folks at least sketched out pre-game (with a lot of gaps for the insertion and hooks for the insertion of Mentors/Allies/Contacts and the like). A lot of studying the history of city in question and picking up the fun bits to highlight in the background. Oh, and have a general idea of THE STORY or the stories that I was going to be telling. <br /> <br /> I would prep and run a Doomslayers game a lot differently. (Ruleswise, I'd give them extra XP, etc. I'd probably have a Stygian poltico backdrop and set their Backgrounds against that, but a lot more freeform b/c Stygia is really big and complex and there are a lot of available venues in which to operate when not Oblivian-spelunking). And so on....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-17T11:39:58-08:00","id":343125,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">I\u2019m not sure if I can offer strong advice. What I can do though is offer some anecdotes about how my troupe handled some of the topics your questions touched on. <br /> <br /> <b><u>How to get a Circle together?</u></b><br /> I really like the idea of the \u201ccommon death\u201d backdrop. That never occurred to me, to be honest. <br /> <br /> My troupe\u2019s Circle went with the idea that, like in life, folks end up friends with the people they meet. <br /> <br /> Two of characters met on a Byway. Both being Harbingers (Argos-savvy, not the actual Guild) they began a friendly professional chat that led to them hanging around/helping each other. Finally, the third [regular cast] character was reaped by one of the other characters.<br /> <br /> These were established in a one-session prelude framed as a series of vignettes (rather than a continuous narrative). <br /> <br /> <b><u>Legions, Hierarchs, and Renegades?</u></b><br /> We tried to make concepts that could work without factions and then tacked those on at the end to help round out the characters. The three main characters were:<br /> <br /> The Reaper - A Doomed Reaper that uncauled the Goth and befriended the Trucker. He was the \u201cofficial\u201d face of the Circle in Hierarchy territory. <br /> <br /> The Trucker \u2013 A former One-Percent\u2019r who took up truck-driving to clean up his act. His Relic big rig became the Circle\u2019s mascot. A free spirit that thought folks should have the freedom to believe what they want. He was a welcome visitor in several Heretic groups. <br /> <br /> The Goth \u2013 A dead Goth [this was before Emo was thing] that was killed by a vampire and dedicated his afterlife to revenge. His quest involved some activities that lead to a small celebrity status among Renegades; he was the Circle\u2019s liaison in \u201crebel\u201d Necropoli. <br /> <br /> The \u201cmulti-faction\u201d dynamic worked well for the troupe. It did create a wee bit of inter-character tension, but in a fun-not-derailing way. It also let us swap out STs, with that character \u201coff-camera\u201d doing [Faction] stuff. <br /> <br /> <b><u>Passions and Fetters</u></b><br /> As an ST, I love (LOVE LOVE LOVE) Wraith character sheets. Need a subplot? Look at the troupe\u2019s characters sheets. <br /> <br /> Think of a Chronicle like a television series. The main story might be the season\u2019s story arc, but you can have a few episodes that deal with subplot material. Or, you can make those the focus. <br /> <br /> We had a short story arc in which the Circle helped Trucker find his beloved rig and another (longer) story arc that centered on Goth finally killing his fanged nemesis. <br /> <br /> When working with (or around) Passion and Fetters, talk it over with the players. Some players would like to make their character resolutions a focus. Others, not so much. <br /> <br /> <b><u>Illegal Arcanoi</u></b><br /> If it makes the story interesting, go with it. Or, if you have \u201cone of those\u201d players, that illegal status can help reel it in. Otherwise, you can treat it like RL speeding; yes, technically, going one mile over the speed limit is illegal, but very few drivers get pulled over for that.<br /> <br /> Hope this offered some perspective. YMMV.<br /> <br /> </span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-17T12:29:43-08:00","id":343141,"title":"","content":"Yeah, Ajax pretty much answered your first question.<br /> <br /> As for illegal Arcanoi, Heretics and Renegades generally don't care about the laws of the Hierarchy so they're not likely to care all that much. With the Hierarchy it varies between place to place and person to person and the dirty little secret is that pretty much everyone at one point or another makes use of an illegal Arcanoi, either directly or indirectly. Hell during the 4th Great Maelstrom, when the Smiling Lord rebelled he allied himself with the Monitors Guild so they could protect the Fetters of his officers which would mean that those Necropoli under the Grim Legion's control at that time wouldn't treat the Monitors as a Criminal Guild.<br /> <br /> Generally if the wraiths kept things downplayed, avoid violating the Dictum Mortuum in loud, obvious ways, and the like, then they'll have little to worry about the Hierarchy coming to soulforge them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}